{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791809541,"nonce":"t91H4u2o2Oslt6Nr6yjgywa66FA=","message":"Node startup/runtime measurements (2026-10-12), SDK b9eff9cc1321aafe9a9f22d178c0e11a7098c59d, Node 24.2.0 / V8 13.6.233.10-node.17, macOS M1 Pro. Production source and addon unchanged; all instrumentation lives in /private/tmp/blinc-first-frame-startup.\n\nRan 60 fresh processes: 30 baseline cases (six bare-Node/addon-only controls and 24 native windows), 24 paired native GPU/pump timestamp cases, then six cases separating appkit::serve, launch-status query and winit pump_app_events. All completed; the historical 60-second no-show period did not recur. Fresh processes do not imply cold OS/driver caches. Passive variants add no repeating measurement timer before presentation; monitored variants are reported separately because their timers add native wakeups. Only Node 24.2.0 is installed locally, so this is not a Node-version comparison.\n\nMeasured performance.nodeTiming bootstrap, import/loader wall time, process CPU and thread CPU, minor GC, optional event-loop delay and a 100ms timer, GPU native task start/completion and JS promise-handler delivery. Baseline bootstrap median about 11-12ms (one 82.9ms control outlier); bare Node timer lateness median 0.36ms, addon-only 0.63ms, both under 1.5ms. Native-window 100ms timer is commonly 109-119ms overdue while the synchronous window constructor occupies roughly 190ms. GC totals are approximately 0.8-2ms, all observed minor GC.\n\nIn the native-timestamp passive text-scene comparison (three fresh runs per mode), device creation takes 2.98ms median with the libuv bridge and 3.20ms with the 16ms polling fallback. Delay from the native device signal to the JS device promise handler is 68.27ms with the bridge versus 0.158ms with polling. First presented text frame from script start is 309.64ms versus 322.88ms, respectively: faster promise delivery does not automatically yield an earlier visible frame because AppKit exposure is still pending. Across clear and text passive cases, bridge callback delivery median 68.54ms versus polling 0.155ms.\n\nDeeper timestamps locate the delay in winit pump_app_events, not the launch-status query: the external pump receives timeout zero but its first post-open winit pass takes 57.9-105.8ms, with native GPU completion inside that call and JS delivery immediately after return. LaunchServices launch-status queries are 0.10-0.17ms in these six cases. The earlier synchronous winit launch pass inside window construction takes 91-130ms. Polling also pays the post-open native pass (about 63ms), but only after its device promise has already been delivered. This demonstrates native platform pumping delays Node callbacks during normal startup; it does not establish where inside winit/AppKit that time is spent or prove the cause of the rare 60-second failure.\n\nNode event-loop utilization approaches 100% with the bridge but is not CPU usage: native pump time outside libuv's event provider counts as active. Measured main-thread CPU to baseline text first frame is approximately 144ms against 305ms wall time and includes native calls. A separate V8 CPU/JIT trace attributes much wall time to Window.open and idle; minor GC is 2ms and no deoptimization bailout appears in that one trace. Profiling increases first-frame wall time to about 506ms, so it is not used as a baseline.\n\nThere is also a cold addon-load discrepancy to keep distinct: immediately after replacing each isolated diagnostic binary, first load took 553ms and later 743ms wall time with about 13ms main-thread CPU. Repeated loads of the same binary then took about 5-6ms. The loader/OS cause is not established; those cold-load samples are preserved rather than treated as a JavaScript execution cost.\n\nArtifacts: node-startup-runtime.mjs; runtime-runs/results.json and summary.json; runtime-native-runs/results.json and summary.json; runtime-appkit-runs/results.json and summary.json; runtime-profiles/bridge-scene.cpuprofile and bridge-scene.log. Native instrumentation is memory-buffered and read after presentation. This narrows ordinary first-draw scheduling costs but leaves 748f2d7 open; no production timing or activation workaround was introduced. Node perf_hooks reference: https://nodejs.org/download/release/v24.2.0/docs/api/perf_hooks.html","files":null}]}